[[Category:Pages without translation templates]] <!--enwiki derived wikidata interwikis--> {{Commons category|Keta-jinja}} [[ja:気多神社]] [[zh:氣多神社 (富山縣)]]
[[Category:pages with valid en interwikis 2]]

{{Distinguish|Keta Taisha}}{{Infobox religious building | name = Keta Shrine |native_name = 気多神社 | image = Keta-jinja (Takaoka), shaden.jpg | caption = ''Shaden'' of Keta Shrine {{Infobox mapframe|tyep=shape|zoom=14|frame-width=240}} | map_type = Japan Toyama Prefecture#Japan | map_alt = | coordinates = {{coord|36|48|0.8|N|137|2|39.5|E|region:JP-16|display=inline,title}} | map_relief = 1 | map_size = | map_caption = | religious_affiliation = [[Shinto]] | type = | deity = [[Ōkuninushi]] | founded_by = | established = [[:en:Nara period|Nara period]] | date_destroyed = | location = 1-10-1 Fushikiichinomiya, Takaoka-shi, Toyama-ken | website = | architecture_style = ''[[Nagare-zukuri]]'' | festival = April 18 | footnotes ={{box|background=white|align=center|wide=yes|border size=3px|border color=pink|text align=center|[[:en:Important Cultural Property (Japan)|Important Cultural Property]]}} }} {{nihongo|'''Keta Shrine'''|気多神社|Keta Jinja}} is a [[:en:Shinto shrine|Shinto shrine]] located in the Fushiki-ichinomiya neighborhood of the city of [[:en:Takaoka, Toyama|Takaoka]], [[:en:Toyama Prefecture|Toyama Prefecture]], [[:en:Japan|Japan]]. It one of four shrines claiming the title of ''[[ichinomiya]]'' of former [[:en:Etchū Province|Etchū Province]], and has one of the strongest claims, as it is located in close proximity to the ruins of the [[kokufu|provincial capital]], ''[[:en:Provincial temple|kokubunji]]'' and the [[Sōja shrine|''Sōja'']] of Etchū Province, and the local place name is also ichinomiya". The main festival of the shrine is held annually on April 18.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Shibuya |first1=Nobuhiro |title=Shokoku jinja Ichinomiya Ninomiya San'nomiya |date=2015 |publisher= Yamakawa shuppansha |isbn=4634150867 |language=Japanese}}</ref>
==Enshrined ''kami''== The primary ''[[kami]]'' enshrined at Keta Jinja are: *{{nihongo|[[Ōkuninushi]]|大己貴命 ||}}, who is called "Onamuchi-no-mikoto" at this location * {{nihongo|'''Nunakawa-hime-no-mikoto'''|奴奈加波比売命 ||}}.
The secondary ''kami'' nshrined at Keta Jinja are: * {{nihongo|[[Kukurihime|'''Kukurhime-no-mikoto''']]||菊理姫命 ||}} * {{nihongo| [[Kotoshironushi-no-Mikoto|'''Kotoshironush-no-mikoto''']] |事代主命 ||}}.
==History== The foundation of Keta Jinja is uncertain. The shrine claims that it was founded in 717 AD, but there is no documentary evidence for this. [[:en:Noto Province|Noto Province]] was separated from Etchū in 718, reunited in 741 and separated again in 757. Per historical records, the [[Keta Taisha]] in Noto regarded as the ''ichinomiya'' during this period, but after the final separation of these provinces, a new ''ichinomiya'' needed to be designated within the reduced borders of Etchū. The new shrine was initially called the Shinketa Myōjin, or "New Keta Shrine" in [[:en:Nara period|Nara period]] records, and its designation as ''ichinomiya'' immediately created a conflict with [[Imizu Shrine|Imizu Jinja]], an existing shrine which had previously served as the ''ichinomiya'' during the initial separation of the two provinces.<ref name="Yoshiki">{{cite book |last1=Yoshiki |first1=Emi |title=Zenkoku `Ichinomiya' tettei gaido |date=2007 |publisher=PHP Institute |isbn=4569669301 |language=Japanese}}</ref>
In the 927 AD ''[[Engishiki]]'' records, the shrine is named the "Ichinomiya Keta Shrine" but is listed as the 13th and most recent of the shrines in Imizu District. The conflict between Keta Jinja and Imizu Jinja would continue for centuries and would never be fully resolved. The Kita Shrine was rebuilt in the [[:en:Juei|Juei]] era (1182-1184) by [[:en:Kiso Yoshinaka|Kiso Yoshinaka]] after it had been destroyed by a fire. It was again rebuilt by [[:en:Uesugi Kenshin|Uesugi Kenshin]] in the [[:en:Eiroku|Eiroku]] era (1558-1570). Under the [[:en:Edo Period|Edo Period]] [[Tokugawa shogunate]], Etchū came under the control of the [[Maeda clan]], who continued to patronize the shrine until the [[Meiji Restoration|Meiji restoration]].<ref name="Yoshiki"/>
With the establishment of [[:en:State Shinto|State Shinto]], the shrine was designated as a {{nihongo|prefectural shrine|県社|lt=Ken-sha|1=Modern_system_of_ranked_Shinto_shrines#Metropolitan_and_Prefectural_Shrines||}}. In 1931, the ''[[Honden]]'' of the shrine, dating from the [[:en:Muromachi period|Muromachi period]], was designated a National [[:en:Important Cultural Property (Japan)|Important Cultural Property]].
The shrine is located a 15-minute walk from [[:en:Etchū-Kokubu Station|Etchū-Kokubu Station]] on the [[:en:West Japan Railway Company|West Japan Railway Company]] [[:en:Himi Line|Himi Line]].<ref name="Okada">{{cite book |last1=Okada |first1=Shoji |title=Taiyō no chizuchō 24 zenkoku `Ichinomiya' meguri |date=2014 |publisher=Heibonsha |isbn=4582945619 |language=Japanese}}</ref>
==Gallery== <gallery> Keta-jinja (Takaoka), honden.jpg|Honden (ICP) File:Keta Shrine.jpeg|Entrance File:Keta-jinja (Takaoka), haiden.jpg|Haiden File:県社気多神社.jpg|Keta Jinja in 1909 </gallery>
==Cultural Properties== ===Important Cultural Properties=== *{{nihongo|'''''Honden'''''|本殿||}}, Muromachi period, dated 1467–1572, designated a National Important Cultural Property in 1931.<ref name="Bunka1">{{cite web |url=https://kunishitei.bunka.go.jp/heritage/detail/102/720|title=気多神社本殿|trans-title=Keta Jinja Honden|language=Japanese |publisher=[[Agency for Cultural Affairs]] |accessdate=August 20, 2020}}</ref>
==See also== *[[:en:List of Shinto shrines|List of Shinto shrines]] *[[Ichinomiya]] *Other shrines claiming to be [[:en:Etchū Province|Etchū]] Ichinomiya ** {{ill|Imizu Shrine|en|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Imizu Shrine|qq=}} ** {{ill|Takase Shrine|en|qq=draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takase Shrine|qq=}} ** {{ill|Oyama Shrine (Tateyama)|lt=Oyama Shrine|simple|qq=draft|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Oyama Shrine (Tateyama)|qq=}}
